.TH CJSON_DICTIONARY_SET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_dictionary_set \- set or replace a key in an object node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_dictionary_set(cjson obj, const char *key, cjson child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Inserts or replaces the value for key in obj.  If a previous child
existed under key, the new child is stored first and the previous
child is deep-freed only once it is safely in place, so a failed
insert never leaves the slot dangling.

Ownership of child transfers to obj on every outcome that touches
child at all: on success the object owns child; on a failure caused
by obj/key being invalid (obj is NULL or not CJSON_DICTIONARY, key is
NULL) or by an internal insertion failure, a freshly unattached child
is deep-freed by cjson_dictionary_set and must not be freed by the
caller.  The one class of failure that leaves child completely
untouched, still owned by whatever it was already attached to, is an
already-attached child (see below); this holds regardless of what
else is wrong with the call, so an invalid obj/key never causes an
already-attached child to be destroyed.

child must not already be attached to a list/dictionary parent, and
must not already contain obj somewhere within its own subtree, with
one exception: passing the exact node already stored under key back
in (e.g. cjson_dictionary_set(obj, k, cjson_dictionary_get(obj, k)))
is a harmless no-op that returns ccol_success without modifying obj.
Any other already-attached child (stored under a different key, in
a different container, or obj itself) is rejected with
ccol_invalid_args, leaving child completely untouched and still owned
by whatever it was already attached to.  Likewise, if obj is already
reachable by descending from child (attaching child would then make
obj both a new ancestor of child and an existing descendant of it, a
cycle), the call fails with ccol_invalid_args; if that check cannot be
completed under memory pressure, ccol_not_enough_memory is returned
instead of silently risking an undetected cycle.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
obj    Target dictionary node (must be CJSON_DICTIONARY).
key    Null-terminated key string; a copy is stored internally.
child  Value node; must not already be attached elsewhere, and must
       not already contain obj in its own subtree (see above).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full if obj has already reached its maximum element
count.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A node returned by cjson_get(), cjson_list_get(), or
cjson_dictionary_get() is a borrowed reference into an existing tree,
not a freshly owned node, and must never be passed as child unless it
is the value already stored under key (see above).  Use cjson_clone()
first to obtain an independent copy that is safe to store elsewhere.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_dictionary_get (3),
.BR cjson_create_dictionary (3),
.BR cjson_clone (3)
